course: dictionary
lesson: 8689
title: Machines that record earthquakes
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: A seismograph is a machine that measures and records how much the ground shakes. Its name comes from two old Greek words: seismos means shaking, and graph means writing or drawing. So a seismograph writes down the shaking.
explain: In science lessons, a seismograph shows the shaking as a zigzag line on paper or a screen. Bigger zigzags mean stronger shaking. Read this sentence: «Earthquakes send out fast P waves that push and pull, slower S waves that shake side to side, and surface waves that do most damage; a machine called a seismograph records them.»
explain: The clue is the word «records». The machine writes down the waves. Swap your meaning into the sentence: "a machine called a seismograph measures and records them" still makes sense, so you have found the right meaning.
fact: A seismograph measures and records how much the ground shakes during an earthquake.
fact: A «seismograph reading» is the zigzag record that the machine makes of the shaking.
fact: Scientists use seismographs to find where an earthquake happened and how strong it was.
fact: P waves are fast and push and pull; S waves are slower and shake side to side.
fact: Surface waves do most of the damage in an earthquake.
exercise: «Earthquakes send out fast P waves that push and pull, slower S waves that shake side to side, and surface waves that do most damage; a machine called a seismograph records them.» What does a seismograph do? | Makes earthquakes happen | Stops the ground from moving | Keeps buildings warm | *Measures and records how much the ground shakes
exercise: On a seismograph, what does a bigger zigzag mean? | The machine is broken | No shaking at all | Weaker shaking | *Stronger shaking
exercise: Which waves shake the ground side to side? | The seismograph | P waves | *S waves | Surface waves
test: Which word in «a machine called a seismograph records them» tells you what the seismograph does? | *records | machine | called | them
test: What do scientists use a seismograph to find out about an earthquake? | *Where it happened and how strong it was | Whether it will rain tomorrow | What colour the sky is | How many birds are nearby
exam: Which waves do most of the damage in an earthquake? | Slower waves that shake side to side | *Surface waves | Fast waves that push and pull | A seismograph reading
